.Dd September 24, 2026
.Dt DSPROMOTE 8
.Os NextBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm dspromote ,
.Nm dsdemote ,
.Nm dsjoin ,
.Nm dsleave ,
.Nm dsstatus
.Nd make this machine a directory server, or bind it to one
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm dspromote
.Op Fl v
.Nm dsdemote
.Op Fl v
.Nm dsjoin
.Op Fl v
.Ar server
.Nm dsleave
.Op Fl v
.Nm dsstatus
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These five commands move a machine between three roles.
.Bl -tag -width "standalone"
.It Li standalone
Accounts live in
.Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices .
Nothing is exported and nothing is mounted.
This is how a machine ships.
.It Li server
The accounts and the home directories are served over NFS and advertised over
Bonjour as
.Li _nextbsd-ds._tcp .
.It Li client
Accounts come from a server, and its home directories are mounted at
.Pa /Network/Users .
.El
.Pp
They do not manage accounts.
.Xr adduser 8 ,
.Xr pw 8 ,
.Xr passwd 1
and
.Xr chpass 1
write the property lists directly, on a server and a standalone machine alike.
.Ss What promotion shares
Promotion moves nothing.
The property lists stay in
.Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices
on every machine in every role, and
.Nm dspromote
shares that directory and
.Pa /Local/Users
over NFS.
.Pp
.Xr nss_directory_services 8
prefers
.Pa /Network/Library/DirectoryServices
when that exists and computes a home directory from whichever file it read,
which is what lets one layout serve every role without rearranging anything.
Nothing mounts
.Pa /Network
on a server, so it reads
.Pa /Local
and its own home directories are under
.Pa /Local/Users ,
where they belong.
A client mounts the server's
.Pa /Local
paths onto its own
.Pa /Network ,
reads
.Pa /Network ,
and finds its home directories under
.Pa /Network/Users .
.Pp
No symbolic link is created, and nothing under
.Pa /Network
is touched on a server.
An earlier version moved the property lists to
.Pa /Network
and left a link behind, on the reasoning that a server had to read the very
file it served.
That does not follow: an NFS export path and the client's mount point are
independent, so a client can mount the server's
.Pa /Local
path at its own
.Pa /Network
path and nothing needs to move.
.Ss What makes a machine a server
.Pa Domain.plist
in
.Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices ,
written by
.Nm dspromote
and removed by
.Nm dsdemote .
It survives a reboot, an unmounted
.Pa /Network
and a stopped
.Xr nfsd 8 ,
none of which change what a machine is.
.Pp
It is the same marker at the same path that Gershwin's
.Nm dscli
uses, and its presence is the whole of the value: that tool writes an empty
dictionary there, so a machine promoted by either one is a server to both.
Contents are never read, and any file at that path makes this a server.
.Ss How every command decides
One question, asked in one order, the first answer winning.
.Bl -tag -width "/Network/Library/DirectoryServices/Domain.plist" -offset indent
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Domain.plist
This machine is the server.
.Pa /Network
is not consulted at all.
.It Pa /Network/Library/DirectoryServices/Domain.plist
This machine is bound to one.
.It neither
Standalone.
.El
.Pp
The server is asked about first because the whole of
.Pa /Local
is exported, so its own marker arrives under
.Pa /Network
along with everything else.
Asked in the wrong order, the one machine that owns the accounts would report
itself somebody else's client and
.Xr adduser 8 ,
.Xr rmuser 8 ,
.Xr pw 8 ,
.Xr passwd 1
and
.Xr chpass 1
would all refuse to manage them.
.Pp
Testing the marker rather than
.Pa Users.plist
also means a client whose server has been demoted reads as standalone at once:
the marker leaves the export and nothing has to notify the client.
An unmounted
.Pa /Network
is standalone too, deliberately \[em] the tools then behave exactly as they
would on a machine that never joined.
.Nm dsleave
is the one exception, so that a client which cannot reach its server can still
be unbound.
.Ss What each command writes
.Bl -column "/etc/exports" "dspromote" "dsdemote" -offset indent
.It Sy Artefact Ta Sy Written by Ta Sy Removed by
.It the block in Pa /etc/exports Ta dspromote Ta dsdemote
.It Pa Domain.plist Ta dspromote Ta dsdemote
.It the Bonjour record Ta dspromote Ta dsdemote
.It Pa Binding.plist Ta dsjoin Ta dsleave
.It the block in Pa /etc/ntp.conf Ta dsjoin Ta emptied by dsleave
.It rpcbind, mountd, nfsd Ta dspromote Ta dsdemote
.It network-mount Ta dsjoin Ta dsleave
.El
.Pp
The kernel permits one default export per filesystem, so
.Pa /etc/exports
groups the exported directories by device.
On the usual layout that is a single line.
.Pp
Both
.Pa /etc/exports
and
.Pa /etc/ntp.conf
are the administrator's files: only the block between the
.Dq BEGIN directory server
and
.Dq END directory server
markers is written, and every other line survives.
.Nm dsdemote
removes the exports block, markers included, and removes the file itself only
when the block was all it held.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl v
Report each artefact as it is written or removed.
.El
.Sh REFUSALS
.Nm dsjoin
refuses on a machine that is already bound, naming the server, rather than
rebinding.
Leaving implicitly would unmount home directories from under anyone logged in.
.Pp
.Nm dspromote
refuses on a machine that is already a server or is bound to one, and
.Nm dsdemote
and
.Nm dsleave
refuse where there is nothing to undo.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/Local/Library/Preferences/mDNSResponder/Services" -compact
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices
the accounts, on every machine in every role
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Domain.plist
the marker.
Present only on a server
.It Pa /Network/Library/DirectoryServices
the mount on a client, absent otherwise
.It Pa /Network/Library/DirectoryServices/Domain.plist
the server's marker, seen through that mount
.It Pa /Local/Users
the home directories
.It Pa /Network/Users
the mount on a client, absent otherwise
.It Pa /etc/exports
the managed block only
.It Pa /etc/ntp.conf
the managed block only
.It Pa /Local/Library/Preferences/mDNSResponder/Services
the Bonjour record
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 when the machine is in the wrong role or an artefact could not
be written, and 2 for a usage error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Make this machine a server, and see what it did:
.Pp
.Dl # dspromote -v
.Dl # dsstatus
.Pp
Bind another machine to it, then undo both:
.Pp
.Dl # dsjoin server.local
.Dl # dsleave
.Dl # dsdemote
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chpass 1 ,
.Xr passwd 1 ,
.Xr exports 5 ,
.Xr adduser 8 ,
.Xr mountd 8 ,
.Xr pw 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Joseph Maloney
.Sh CAVEATS
A bound client cannot change any password: the property lists are exported
read-only and root is squashed.
.Pp
A client whose server is unreachable may have no local account to log in with.
.Pp
Any machine on the network can advertise
.Li _nextbsd-ds._tcp .
There is no authentication of the server.
.Pp
.Nm dsdemote
does not notify bound clients; they stay bound and fail to mount.
